            <ArticleTitle>[Atypical abdominal pain in the first trimester of pregnancy].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>204-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jgyn.2007.12.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 22-year-old woman who presented a violent epigastric pain at eight-weeks gestation. Superior mesenteric vein thrombosis was detected, with an extension to portal vein and remaining blood flow. Screening for thrombophilia revealed a heterozygote prothrombin gene mutation. Portal vein thrombosis is uncommon and difficult to diagnose. Diagnosis is made by Doppler ultrasound, a second intention test to be done in case of unusual upper abdominal pain during pregnancy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
